consider fixed AC described by the two piece-wise linear functions below:

         eff. AC|  /                      eff. AC|  /
                | /                              | /
            x0  |/                         x1    |/
        <----+--------AC seen            <--+----------AC seen
               /|                              / |
              / |                            /   |
         -----  +y0                       --     +y1
                |                                |
                function F0                      function F1
these functions are characterized by the following formulas and may be
considered to approximate to varying degrees a wide variety of functions.

eff. AC = AC seen, x0 < AC seen;   eff. AC = AC seen     , 0 < AC seen;
        = y0     , AC seen < x0;           = AC seen x a1, x1 < AC seen < 0;
                                           = y1          , AC seen < x1;
------------ F0 -----------        ------------ F1 ------------

better approximations to certain functions are obtained by adding more
segments but you knew that. for these functions there is a limit, yi, which
describes the practical limit to the fixed AC protection. you approach that
limit at various rates depending on how you choose your segments, etc.
beyond the math, when you use schemes like this to describe eff. AC there
comes a point when you can say to yourself "i don't need to worry about
the monster hitting me because, at best, it will only do minor damage and
not get a magic attack, i'm kinda invincible". this would be true (and
useful) against the wizard of yendor, as well. you could argue for limits
of -20 or -40 or -60, each has a distinct flavor, but the issue remains
whether your AC rating should be fixed or have a random factor thrown in.
all fixed AC rating schemes suffer from invincibility syndrome.

the eff. AC schemes using a random factor directly attack the invincibility
problem by introducing an uncertainty principle[ref. prev. posts]. however,
this need not be an either/or proposition. the fixed rag[ref. prev. posts]
was suggested as a means to cover this schism. there may be other solutions
as well. the cloak of random protection could be designed to fall apart
after it was worn for x number of moves (with appropriate hints that it
seems to fraying). this lets the wearer decide when it is most oppurtune
to have fixed AC protection. repeatedly wearing/taking off the rag could
shorten its lifetime also. perhaps blessed mummy wrappings could serve
(preserve?) this purpose without the need to introduce a new artifact
into the game (only your oracle knows for sure).

the random AC scheme is doable as is, but could get amended in an attempt
to lower the frustration threshold perceived by certain players.
